[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Annotations in Core Java: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Annotations, a form of metadata, provide data about a program that is not part of the program itself. They have no direct effect on the operation of the code they annotate. Annotations can be used by the compiler for compile-time detection of errors or suppression of warnings, and by runtime libraries and tools to process and handle code in a specific manner. This tutorial covers built-in annotations, custom annotations, and how to access them using reflection."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Built-in Annotations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides several built-in annotations that are commonly used. Let's explore some of the most important ones:"},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"@Override"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@Override` annotation informs the compiler that the element is meant to override an element declared in a superclass. If a method marked with `@Override` does not actually override a method in a superclass, the compiler will generate an error."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Generic animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"@Deprecated"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@Deprecated` annotation marks a program element as one that should no longer be used. Compilers generate a warning whenever a program uses a method, class, or field marked with the `@Deprecated` annotation. This annotation is useful for indicating that a feature is obsolete and should be replaced with a newer alternative."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class MyClass {
    @Deprecated
    public void oldMethod() {
        System.out.println("This method is deprecated.");
    }

    public void newMethod() {
        System.out.println("Use this method instead.");
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"@SuppressWarnings"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@SuppressWarnings` annotation instructs the compiler to suppress specific warnings that it would otherwise generate. This is useful when you know a warning is irrelevant in a particular context. Common warning types include `unchecked`, `deprecation`, `fallthrough`, and `serial`."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SuppressWarningsExample {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List list = new ArrayList(); // Compiler would normally warn about raw type usage
        list.add("Hello");
        System.out.println(list.get(0));
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Annotations"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define your own annotations to provide custom metadata for your code. Custom annotations are declared using the `@interface` keyword. They can contain elements, which are similar to methods, that define the data the annotation holds. Annotations can also have meta-annotations that specify how the annotation itself should be handled."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining a Custom Annotation"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import java.lang.annotation.ElementType;
import java.lang.annotation.Retention;
import java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy;
import java.lang.annotation.Target;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface MyAnnotation {
    String author() default "Unknown";
    String date();
    int revision() default 1;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)` specifies that the annotation should be retained at runtime, making it accessible through reflection."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@Target(ElementType.METHOD)` specifies that the annotation can only be applied to methods."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`author()`, `date()`, and `revision()` are elements of the annotation. `author` and `revision` have default values."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Using a Custom Annotation"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public class MyClass {
    @MyAnnotation(author = "John Doe", date = "2023-10-27", revision = 2)
    public void myMethod() {
        System.out.println("Method annotated with MyAnnotation");
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"heading","text":"Reflection and Annotations"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection allows you to inspect and manipulate classes, interfaces, fields, and methods at runtime. You can use reflection to access annotations and their values. This is particularly useful for frameworks and libraries that need to process annotations dynamically."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Accessing Annotations at Runtime"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"import java.lang.reflect.Method;

public class AnnotationProcessor {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Class<MyClass> clazz = MyClass.class;
        Method method = clazz.getMethod("myMethod");

        if (method.isAnnotationPresent(MyAnnotation.class)) {
            MyAnnotation annotation = method.getAnnotation(MyAnnotation.class);
            System.out.println("Author: " + annotation.author());
            System.out.println("Date: " + annotation.date());
            System.out.println("Revision: " + annotation.revision());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`clazz.getMethod("myMethod")` retrieves the `myMethod` method using reflection."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`method.isAnnotationPresent(MyAnnotation.class)` checks if the method is annotated with `MyAnnotation`."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`method.getAnnotation(MyAnnotation.class)` retrieves the `MyAnnotation` instance."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Finally, we access the annotation's elements (author, date, revision) using the corresponding methods."},{"order":33,"type":"heading","text":"Meta-Annotations"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Meta-annotations are annotations that apply to other annotations. They provide information about the annotation itself. The most common meta-annotations are `@Target`, `@Retention`, `@Documented`, and `@Inherited`."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"@Target"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Specifies the types of program elements to which an annotation can be applied. `ElementType` enum defines the possible targets (e.g., `METHOD`, `TYPE`, `FIELD`)."},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"@Retention"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Specifies how long the annotation should be retained. `RetentionPolicy` enum defines the retention policies (`SOURCE`, `CLASS`, `RUNTIME`)."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"@Documented"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Indicates that annotations with this type should be documented by javadoc and similar tools."},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"@Inherited"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"Indicates that an annotation type is automatically inherited. If you apply an `@Inherited` annotation to a class, any subclasses will also inherit the annotation."},{"order":43,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"Annotations are a powerful feature in Java that allows you to add metadata to your code. Built-in annotations provide standard functionality, while custom annotations enable you to define your own metadata. Reflection allows you to access and process annotations at runtime, making them a valuable tool for building flexible and extensible applications."}]
